WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.110 --> 00:00:06.880
[Narrator] In one year, Disney
grossed over $2.7 billion

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:11.880
on "Avengers: Endgame"
and lost $170 million

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:15.173
on "Dark Phoenix" and other titles.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:20.620
Turns out that in the 2010s,
not even bankable stars,

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:24.330
big marketing campaigns, or impressive CGI

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:27.280
can guarantee box office success.

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:30.660
We're going to take a look
at exactly what went wrong

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:35.220
with some of the most expensive
movie flops of the decade.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:38.120
We've drawn up a list
using reported losses

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:40.820
from various media outlets.

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:42.677
"I am telling you, I am nobody."

00:00:42.677 --> 00:00:44.370
[Narrator] "Jupiter Ascending."

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:46.890
The problem wasn't its
cast members Mila Kunis

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:49.530
and Channing Tatum, or
even the bizarre plot

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:53.450
of a cleaning lady with an
interplanetary inheritance.

00:00:53.450 --> 00:00:55.620
It was overspending.

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:58.620
Directors the Wachowskis
had previously worked

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:01.210
with Studio 8 President Jeff Robinov

00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:04.170
on the 1999 hit "The Matrix."

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:07.380
Robinov signed off on
casting and production design

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:10.660
and approved a huge
budget but left the studio

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:13.030
before the films' completion.

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:17.200
Fixing VFX issues prerelease
also added to the mix.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:19.090
The movie opened to poor reviews,

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:21.723
and you can guess what happened next.

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:25.040
Name a film from the past decade featuring

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:28.950
anthropomorphised,
shape-shifting cars and trucks.

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:32.420
You're probably thinking
of a "Transformers" movie.

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:34.790
And there's a reason why you wouldn't know

00:01:34.790 --> 00:01:38.670
the bomb "Monster Trucks,"
about a high-school senior

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:41.740
who discovers a creature
that feeds on oil.

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:43.890
Part of its failure
can be blamed on a lack

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:46.370
of on-screen talent, with Lucas Till

00:01:46.370 --> 00:01:48.320
not providing much of an audience draw.

00:01:49.456 --> 00:01:53.120
Also, in the age of
successful animated features,

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:56.550
targeting the kids market
with a live-action cars movie

00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:59.030
that wasn't based on a
well-known Nickelodeon

00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:01.863
or Hasbro property was a big risk.

00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:04.320
"I like a good adventure."

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.370
"I'm looking for an adventure of my own."

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:10.230
[Narrator] This movie not
only changed its title months

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:14.460
before opening but also
underwent a change in director.

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:16.107
The movie was originally titled

00:02:16.107 --> 00:02:19.370
"Jack the Giant Killer," which
suggests that it was planned

00:02:19.370 --> 00:02:22.147
to be a grittier, edgier
retelling of Roald Dahl's

00:02:22.147 --> 00:02:24.058
"Jack and the Beanstalk."

00:02:24.058 --> 00:02:27.990
It also suffered from a
glut of similar releases.

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:30.077
It came out shortly after Jeremy Renner's

00:02:30.077 --> 00:02:32.190
"Hansel &amp; Gretel: Witch Hunters,"

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:34.423
another childlike fantasy flick.

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:37.230
"You didn't eat your broccoli."

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:38.813
"No broccoli, no TV."

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:42.390
[Narrator] Disney's animated feature sold

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:44.650
just $6.9 million

00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:48.330
in tickets in its North
America opening weekend.

00:02:48.330 --> 00:02:50.950
The plot line of a child
whose mother is taken away

00:02:50.950 --> 00:02:54.450
from him was off-putting
for family audiences,

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:57.480
as was the unique animation style.

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:01.600
Producer Robert Zemeckis opted
for motion capture instead

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:05.400
of hand-drawn or Pixar
style of computer animation,

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:07.240
which led to comments about characters'

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:10.763
unnatural facial expressions
being terrifying.

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:14.150
"I long for the day that
I can come back for you"

00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:15.820
"and explain everything."

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:18.950
[Narrator] By 2015,
starring in crowd pleasers

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:22.360
such as "Les Miserables"
and the "X-Men" franchise

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:25.210
had to put Hugh Jackman
in a bankable position

00:03:25.210 --> 00:03:27.210
as a leading man.

00:03:27.210 --> 00:03:29.240
But this adaptation of the classic

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:33.540
JM Barrie children's story
"Peter Pan" was criticized

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:37.810
for its simple plot and
pantomime performances.

00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:40.160
After flopping with domestic audiences,

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:41.540
it went on to underperform

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:44.233
in the world's second-largest
film market: China.

00:03:45.070 --> 00:03:46.010
Why?

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:47.440
It was released during a summer

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:50.060
of competitive family-friendly films,

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:53.480
such as French animated
feature "The Little Prince"

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:55.670
and Marvel's "Ant-Man."

00:03:55.670 --> 00:03:56.593
Poor Hugh.

00:03:57.494 --> 00:03:59.994
"It is inevitable."

00:04:01.587 --> 00:04:03.360
[Narrator] "King Arthur:
Legend of the Sword"

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:05.400
was panned by critics

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:09.110
and made just $15 million
in its opening weekend.

00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:12.083
This would be manageable for
a small-budget British movie,

00:04:12.083 --> 00:04:14.820
but it was the exact opposite:

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:17.130
a Guy Ritchie-directed Warner Bros. flick

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:19.300
that cost millions.

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:21.990
So why did it tank so bad?

00:04:21.990 --> 00:04:24.490
Idris Elba and Colin Farrell are rumored

00:04:24.490 --> 00:04:26.090
to have turned down roles,

00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:28.493
leaving Jude Law as the only A-lister.

00:04:29.510 --> 00:04:31.330
And the story wasn't new.

00:04:31.330 --> 00:04:34.790
Home audiences were already
familiar with an adaption

00:04:34.790 --> 00:04:37.690
of the King Arthur legend
starring Clive Owen

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:39.213
which flopped in 2004.

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:43.095
Nowadays he's the internet's boyfriend.

00:04:43.095 --> 00:04:45.000
"You're breathtaking."

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:48.380
[Narrator] But in 2013,
Keanu Reeves hadn't had a hit

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:52.110
since 2003's "The Matrix Revolutions."

00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:56.020
Samurai fantasy-adventure
"47 Ronin" followed a string

00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:59.250
of misses for the star,
such as "Constantine"

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:01.950
and "The Day the Earth Stood Still."

00:05:01.950 --> 00:05:04.270
The movie's release date didn't help,

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:07.280
competing in a December
box office already packed

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:09.840
with "The Wolf of Wall Street,"

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:12.597
the "Hobbit" sequel, "American Hustle,"

00:05:12.597 --> 00:05:15.722
"Saving Mr. Banks," and "Anchorman 2."

00:05:15.722 --> 00:05:17.290
"Boy."

00:05:17.290 --> 00:05:19.810
[Narrator] You'd need to
be a committed movie buff

00:05:19.810 --> 00:05:23.430
to watch all those releases
over the festive period.

00:05:23.430 --> 00:05:25.713
But we love you no matter what, Keanu.

00:05:27.010 --> 00:05:30.440
Producer Peter Jackson of
"Lord of the Rings" fame taking

00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:34.230
on a beloved seven-book
series by Philip Reeve sounds

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:36.500
like a license to print money.

00:05:36.500 --> 00:05:39.137
But "Mortal Engines" took a crushingly low

00:05:39.137 --> 00:05:44.137
$7.5 million across 3,103 theaters

00:05:44.470 --> 00:05:46.373
at the domestic box office.

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:49.930
There were some obvious
reasons it flopped:

00:05:49.930 --> 00:05:52.610
lack of star power, confusing narrative

00:05:52.610 --> 00:05:54.760
about cannibalistic megacities,

00:05:54.760 --> 00:05:58.530
and a woman with a mysterious destiny.

00:05:58.530 --> 00:05:59.950
But what really crushed it

00:05:59.950 --> 00:06:02.690
was the surprising box office smashes

00:06:02.690 --> 00:06:04.980
of release-adjacent movies.

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:09.130
Clint Eastwood's "The
Mule" made $17.5 million

00:06:09.130 --> 00:06:11.920
in its opening, one of the highest ever

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:13.760
for an Eastwood movie.

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:15.690
And family audiences were drawn

00:06:15.690 --> 00:06:18.360
to "Spider-Man: Into the Spider-Verse,"

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:21.238
which piggybacked on the superhero trend.

00:06:21.238 --> 00:06:23.180
"That's a copy."

00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:24.880
[Narrator] There are
negative movie reviews,

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:27.740
and then there are the reviews
that "The Lone Ranger" got

00:06:27.740 --> 00:06:29.850
on its opening weekend.

00:06:29.850 --> 00:06:33.260
On paper, it sounded great:
reuniting Johnny Depp

00:06:33.260 --> 00:06:35.620
with the team behind
"Pirates of the Caribbean"

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:38.790
and adding Armie Hammer,
who was a rising star thanks

00:06:38.790 --> 00:06:41.240
to "The Social Network."

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:44.740
But critics called this 149-minute outing

00:06:44.740 --> 00:06:47.400
too long and clunky.

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:49.710
The Guardian reported
that the filmmakers tried

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:52.550
to scale down the budget
after being spooked

00:06:52.550 --> 00:06:54.920
by other modern Westerns bombing,

00:06:54.920 --> 00:06:58.810
such as 2011's "Cowboys &amp; Aliens."

00:06:58.810 --> 00:07:00.910
But the budget soon spiraled again,

00:07:00.910 --> 00:07:04.553
to the tune of $215 million.

00:07:06.340 --> 00:07:07.770
Coming in at number one

00:07:07.770 --> 00:07:09.970
is one of the biggest box office flops

00:07:09.970 --> 00:07:13.657
of all time: Disney's "John Carter."

00:07:14.930 --> 00:07:18.510
What really killed this
one was the trailers.

00:07:18.510 --> 00:07:21.520
First, was a teaser
featuring a morose cover

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:24.210
of Arcade Fire's "My Body is a Cage,"

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:26.538
which featured no real action.

00:07:26.538 --> 00:07:31.043
♪ From dancing with the one I love ♪

00:07:31.043 --> 00:07:33.140
[Narrator] Further trailers
displayed the movie

00:07:33.140 --> 00:07:36.660
as the most generic, boring blockbuster.

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:41.050
It had no real star power and
a glut of special effects.

00:07:41.050 --> 00:07:44.250
Andrew Stanton, who also
directed "Finding Nemo"

00:07:44.250 --> 00:07:48.532
and "Wall-E," reportedly
overrode Disney marketing execs

00:07:48.532 --> 00:07:52.240
to have the final say on
the promotional material.

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:55.330
Which, let's be honest, was an error.

00:07:55.330 --> 00:07:58.777
Even the "of Mars" was
ditched from the title.

00:07:58.777 --> 00:08:02.637
The movie is based on Edgar
Rice Burroughs's 1917 story

00:08:02.637 --> 00:08:04.370
"A Princess of Mars."

00:08:04.370 --> 00:08:08.760
But just "John Carter" was as
vague as it was going to get.

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:10.895
Critics weren't kind to it either.

00:08:10.895 --> 00:08:15.613
It went on to eventually
lose close to $224 million.

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:19.550
A little nod here to the also-rans,

00:08:19.550 --> 00:08:23.280
including "Deepwater
Horizon," "How Do You Know,"

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:25.210
and "The Promise."

00:08:25.210 --> 00:08:28.270
Did you go to see any of
these box office disasters?

00:08:28.270 --> 00:08:29.437
Comment below.

